<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mastia</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Mastia Tarseion</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mastia was an Iberian town in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, chief town of the Mastieni. It appears as Mastia Tarseion in the second treaty between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>, of 348 BCE, recorded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a>, marking the limit beyond which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> might not trade or found cities. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthago-nova">Carthago Nova</a>, modern Cartagena, was founded around 227 BCE probably on or near its site.</p></div>
